4. `\section*{}` (unnumbered sections) won't appear unless manually added with `\addcontentsline`

The TOC will automatically list sections with page numbers. For books, use `\chapter` in a `book`/`report` class. Figures/tables can be added to their own lists using `\listoffigures`/`\listoftables`.

lsp.reference:
To generate a table of contents automatically in a LaTeX document, you can use the `\tableofcontents` command. Here's an example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\begin{document}

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}
This is the introduction section.

\subsection{Background}
This is the background subsection.

\section{Methodology}
This is the methodology section.

\subsection{Data Collection}
This is the data collection subsection.

\subsection{Data Analysis}
This is the data analysis subsection.

\section{Conclusion}
This is the conclusion section.

\end{document}
```

In this example, the `\tableofcontents` command is used to generate the table of contents. The `\section` and `\subsection` commands are used to define the sections and subsections, respectively. LaTeX will automatically generate the table of contents based on these commands.

Note: You may need to compile the document twice to see the table of contents updated correctly.

